// Dll/Enemy/EnemySlots.inc - the enemy interface vtable, one line per slot in
// slot order (the order GCC assigned them, so declaration order matters).
//
//   ENEMY_SLOT(offset, return type, name, (params), base)
//
// base: 1 = EnemyObjBase implements it (address in the EnemyObjBase*.cpp
// sources), 0 = pure in EnemyObjBase too. Slots +0x000/+0x004 (the
// destructors) are declared by the classes themselves. IEnemyObj (dllfcskl
// 308 slots) ends at +0x4CC; +0x4D0/+0x4D4
// (ENEMY_SLOT_BASE) are new in EnemyObjBase (310 slots).
//
// Names are inferred (most are named after the member they reach); parameter
// lists come from register use in dllfcskl and in the module overrides of all
// 43 enemy modules (argument 1 = this, ints in argument 2.., floats in float argument 1.. taking an integer slot
// each; an 8-byte EnemyVec2 or the 22-byte EnemyShortSet passed by value
// travels in GPRs, a returned struct comes back through a hidden pointer in
// argument 1 with this in argument 2). Getters that end in a plain lwz return unsigned,
// lwz + extsw int. Slots the modules override (the rest never are):
// +0x000/+0x004 (destructors), +0x008..+0x02C, +0x080, +0x084, +0x09C,
// +0x148, +0x150, +0x174, +0x1A0, +0x4D0 (dllskeleton: +0x068).
//
// HB0 / HB1 below are the two hit-box lists (EnemyObjBase::m_hitBoxes0 at
// +0x2B4, m_hitBoxes1 at +0x2C4); the index is not range-checked.

